  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Holafly</strong> if you'd rather pay more upfront to never think about a data meter. Pick <strong>Airalo</strong> if you're comfortable tracking gigabytes in exchange for a lower price. Neither is "better" outright. They're built on opposite pricing philosophies, and which one saves you money depends entirely on how much data you actually use.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Unlimited vs. Pay-Per-GB</h2>
  <p>
    Holafly sells unlimited data as the whole product: one price per country or region, no gigabyte counting. Airalo sells data by the gigabyte, so a light user pays less but has to actually watch usage. For a short trip doing maps, messaging, and some browsing, Airalo is usually cheaper. For a heavy data user, or someone who'd rather overpay a little than risk running out mid-trip, Holafly's flat unlimited price removes that anxiety entirely.
  </p>
  <p>
    Airalo does also sell <strong>unlimited regional eSIMs</strong> (Europe, Asia, North America, Latin America, the Caribbean, MENA, Africa, and Australia), so it's not purely pay-per-GB. But per Airalo's own fair-use policy, those "unlimited" plans run at full speed for the first 3GB each day, then drop to 1Mbps until midnight. Holafly's unlimited plans can also apply a fair-use speed reduction after very heavy same-day usage, though it isn't published as a specific daily GB number the way Airalo's is.
  </p>

