  <h2>Is Holafly or Airalo the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Holafly if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You genuinely don't know how much data you'll use</li>
        <li>Heavy streaming, video calls, or hotspot use</li>
        <li>China is on the itinerary and you want the built-in VPN</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Airalo if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Light user: maps, messaging, some browsing</li>
        <li>Short, single-country trip where you can estimate GB needs</li>
        <li>You'd rather pay less and keep an eye on a data meter</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Holafly's China eSIM has a VPN built in.</strong> Google Maps, WhatsApp, and Gmail just work without installing a separate VPN app first. Airalo has no equivalent; you'd need to sort out your own VPN for China regardless of which Airalo plan you buy.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Airalo's many different discount codes aren't a sign of a messy affiliate program.</strong> They're mostly individual users' Airmoney referral codes, each worth $3 off a first purchase. Holafly's ADAMANDLINDS code, by contrast, is one code that covers both its single-eSIM and monthly-plan products, which most Holafly codes floating around don't.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Holafly vs Airalo FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Holafly or Airalo</h3>
      <p>Depends on usage. Airalo is usually cheaper for light data use on a short trip. Holafly is usually cheaper (or at least less stressful) for heavy data use, since the price doesn't change no matter how much you use.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does either have a real unlimited plan with no cap at all</h3>
      <p>Not quite. Both apply fair-use throttling after heavy same-day use rather than a hard cutoff. Airalo publishes an exact number for its regional unlimited plans (3GB/day full speed, then 1Mbps); Holafly's policy isn't as specific but works the same way in practice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one is better for China</h3>
      <p>Holafly, if you don't want to set up a separate VPN: its China eSIM includes one built in. Airalo has no equivalent, so you'd need your own VPN solution regardless of plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I use one for a multi-country trip and the other for a single-country trip</h3>
      <p>Yes, plenty of travelers do exactly that: Holafly's unlimited regional plans for a data-heavy multi-country leg, Airalo's cheaper per-GB local plans for a light single-country stop.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

